<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hippias of Elis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">5th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hippias of Elis was a Greek sophist of the late 5th century BCE, a contemporary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a>. He served Elis as an envoy and was famous for his wide learning in mathematics, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-astronomy">astronomy</a>, history and the arts, and for his powerful memory. He compiled a list of Olympic victors, and appears in two dialogues attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>, the Hippias Major and Hippias Minor.</p></div>
